# Section 6: Existing Systems, Architectures, and Technical Comparisons

### 147. What sign-language recognition systems already exist?
Systems range from static alphabet classifiers (CNNs) to continuous Sign Language Translation (SLT) frameworks (I3D + Transformer / CTC architectures).

### 148. What ISL recognition systems already exist?
Literature documents numerous academic prototypes using MediaPipe + LSTM/GRU for 20–100 isolated signs, and research models (like AI4Bharat’s INCLUDE benchmarks) using 3D-CNNs (I3D, ResNet3D).

### 149. What WBSL recognition systems already exist?
None that have been formally benchmarked in major IEEE/ACM conferences.

### 150. What BdSL recognition systems already exist?
Systems like *Ishara-Bochon*, *BdSLNet*, and various MobileNet/YOLO character and isolated word recognizers.

### 151. What ASL recognition systems already exist?
State-of-the-art architectures such as **SL-GCN**, **SignBERT+**, **SPOT-ER**, and **Two-Stream Transformers**.

### 152. Which existing systems perform real-time recognition?
MediaPipe landmark-based models running on top of lightweight LSTMs or 1D-CNNs easily achieve real-time frame rates (30+ FPS) on standard CPUs.

### 153. Which existing systems perform continuous recognition?
Connectionist Temporal Classification (CTC) based models (e.g., VAC - Visual Alignment Constraint) and Encoder-Decoder Sequence-to-Sequence models.

### 154. Which existing systems perform sign-to-text translation?
Full SLT frameworks such as **Sign-to-Gloss-to-Text (S2G2T)** pipelines using joint CNN/Transformer backbones.

### 155. Which existing systems produce Bengali text?
Select BdSL research systems; **virtually zero published systems translate ISL/WBSL into Bengali text**.

### 156. Which existing systems use deep learning?
Nearly all modern systems (2018–present) utilize CNNs, RNNs, Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs), or Transformers.

### 157. Which existing systems use computer vision?
All camera-based SLR systems use computer vision (RGB frame analysis, optical flow, or skeletal pose extraction).

### 158. Which existing systems use pose landmarks?
Modern lightweight architectures utilize **MediaPipe Holistic**, **OpenPose**, or **MMPose** coordinates ($x, y, z$ coordinates of keypoints).

### 159. Which existing systems use raw video?
Heavyweight state-of-the-art vision models (e.g., I3D, SlowFast, Video Swin Transformers) process raw RGB frame tensors directly.

### 160. Which existing systems use LSTM?
Standard baseline sequence models commonly stack 2-to-3 layer Bidirectional LSTMs (BiLSTMs) after CNN or landmark feature extraction.

### 161. Which existing systems use Transformer models?
Modern continuous SLT systems employ Spatial-Temporal Transformers or standard Sequence-to-Sequence Transformers (e.g., mBART, MarianMT fine-tuned on glosses).

### 162. Which existing systems use multimodal information?
Advanced models combine RGB video + Optical Flow + Skeletal Pose + Facial Keypoints.

### 163. Which existing systems use language models or LLMs?
Recent translation pipelines feed predicted sign glosses directly into LLMs (like LLaMA-3, Mistral, or GPT-4) prompted for zero-shot or few-shot gloss-to-text generation.

### 164. Which existing systems evaluate unseen signers?
Only high-quality benchmark papers using explicit **Signer-Independent (SI)** validation splits. Many basic papers incorrectly mix frames from the same signer across train and test sets.

### 165. Which existing systems run on low-cost hardware?
Pose-landmark based architectures (e.g., MediaPipe + 2-layer LSTM or small 1D-CNN/MLP) run efficiently on standard consumer laptops and Raspberry Pi devices.

### 166. What limitations do existing systems have?
*   Overfitting to small, studio-recorded, isolated vocabularies.
*   Lack of signer-independent generalization.
*   Total neglect of non-manual markers (facial expressions).
*   Outputting solely English, ignoring regional languages like Bengali.

### 167. Which existing systems are genuinely comparable to my proposed system?
INCLUDE benchmark models (I3D, Pose-based LSTMs) and low-resource Sign-to-Gloss-to-Text pipelines.

### 168. Is there already a system that solves my proposed problem?
No. An end-to-end, real-time, signer-independent system that recognizes ISL/WBSL multimodal signing and outputs grammatically natural **Bengali text** does not exist in current literature.

