# Section 10: Research Gaps, Project Scoping, Hardware, and Ethics

### 233. What is the actual research gap in ISL recognition?
Lack of large-scale continuous datasets, poor signer-independent generalization, and omission of facial non-manual markers.

### 234. What is the actual research gap in WBSL recognition?
A near-total absence of documented digital datasets, baseline models, and linguistic computational studies.

### 235. What is the actual research gap in ISL-to-Bengali translation?
No existing open-source end-to-end framework maps ISL signs directly into grammatically correct Bengali text.

### 236. What is the actual research gap in WBSL-to-Bengali translation?
Unaddressed: zero models or paired gloss-text corpora exist for this language pair.

### 237. What is the actual research gap in continuous ISL recognition?
Solving co-articulation and movement epenthesis on unsegmented video without expensive frame-level manual annotation.

### 238. What is the actual research gap in signer-independent ISL recognition?
Models failing when exposed to unseen signers due to variations in body geometry, signing space, and camera distance.

### 239. What is the actual research gap in multimodal ISL recognition?
Integrating synchronized facial mesh and pose trajectories with hand models rather than relying solely on hand crops.

### 240. What has already been solved for ISL?
Isolated static alphabet classification and small-vocabulary isolated word recognition under controlled, signer-dependent studio conditions.

### 241. What has already been solved for WBSL?
Nothing computationally; only basic sociolinguistic survey mentions exist.

### 242. What has already been solved for BdSL?
Static isolated character recognition (Ishara-Lipi) and small-scale isolated sign classification.

### 243. What has already been solved for ASL?
Large-scale isolated sign recognition (WLASL) and continuous video-to-gloss translation benchmarks (How2Sign).

### 244. Which problems are already well studied?
Isolated alphabet classification using static CNNs (now considered trivial and academically exhausted).

### 245. Which problems are only partially solved?
Isolated word recognition across large vocabularies (>1,000 signs) on unseen signers.

### 246. Which problems have very little research?
Continuous ISL translation into regional Indian languages (e.g., Bengali, Tamil, Telugu).

### 247. Which problems have no publicly available dataset?
Continuous WBSL sentence video paired with native Bengali translations.

### 248. Which problems have no practical system?
Real-time, edge-deployable ISL-to-Bengali continuous translation systems.

### 249. Which problems have no Bengali-language output?
Direct Sign-to-Bengali Translation (SLT) pipelines.

### 250. Which problems have no signer-independent evaluation?
Most published IEEE/Springer conference papers on ISL that report 98%+ accuracy on random splits.

### 251. Which problems have no real-time implementation?
Multimodal continuous Transformer models running locally on non-GPU hardware.

### 252. Which problems are technically feasible for a student project?
A real-time, signer-independent **Isolated-to-Continuous ISL/WBSL Recognition system using MediaPipe + BiLSTM/Transformer**, combined with an **ISL Gloss-to-Bengali NLP translation module**.

### 253. What evidence is required before claiming that a research gap is genuine?
A systematic literature review demonstrating that no peer-reviewed publication has benchmarked the specific language pair, modality, or dataset split under identical conditions.

### 254. Should the project focus on standard ISL or WBSL?
Focus on **Standard ISL as the core model**, augmented with a curated evaluation testbed of **WBSL regional signs**.

### 255. Would focusing on WBSL provide a stronger research gap?
Yes, because WBSL is completely undocumented in AI literature, offering high novelty.

### 256. Would focusing on standard ISL better match the official project title?
Yes. Sticking to standard ISL ensures better access to pre-existing datasets like INCLUDE.

### 257. Would ISL-to-Bengali translation be a meaningful research problem?
Yes. It bridges national sign language with a regional spoken language spoken by over 250 million people.

### 258. Would WBSL-to-Bengali translation be a stronger research problem?
It is the most novel, but data collection will be a major bottleneck.

### 259. Would Bengali output make the project more regionally useful?
Yes, enabling direct communication between Deaf individuals and local administration, healthcare, and education providers in West Bengal.

### 260. Can the project begin with standard ISL and later investigate WBSL?
**Yes, this is the recommended research methodology.** Build the pipeline on ISL, then evaluate domain transfer on WBSL.

### 261. Can regional WBSL signs be treated as an extension of an ISL system?
Yes, via few-shot fine-tuning or transfer learning.

### 262. Should the project explicitly compare ISL and WBSL?
Yes. A comparative evaluation section adds significant academic weight to a thesis.

### 263. Should the project use both standard ISL and West Bengal regional signs?
Yes. A dual-vocabulary approach is ideal.

### 264. What target users should the system serve?
Deaf individuals in West Bengal communicating with hearing Bengali speakers who do not know sign language.

### 265. Should the target users be Deaf people in West Bengal?
Yes.

### 266. Should the system target national ISL users?
It can easily support both by offering a toggle between Bengali and English/Hindi text output.

### 267. Should the system target Bengali-speaking Deaf users specifically?
Yes, focusing on the local linguistic ecosystem.

### 268. What real-world communication problem is the system solving?
Eliminating the reliance on expensive human interpreters for basic daily interactions at banks, hospitals, transport hubs, and government offices in West Bengal.

### 269. What should be the minimum achievable project goal?
Real-time, signer-independent recognition of **50–100 isolated ISL/WBSL signs** using MediaPipe landmarks + LSTM, translated into grammatically correct **Bengali text**.

### 270. What should be the main research question?
*"How can multimodal skeletal landmarks be leveraged to achieve real-time, signer-independent recognition of ISL/WBSL signs and translate them accurately into natural Bengali syntax?"*

### 271. What should be the baseline system?
A standard MediaPipe + Random Forest or basic 2-layer LSTM trained on hand landmarks only, outputting direct word-by-word glosses.

### 272. What should be the proposed system?
A Multimodal (Pose + Hands + Face) Spatio-Temporal Model (e.g., BiLSTM/Transformer) with Signer-Independent validation, connected to an NLP Gloss-to-Bengali translation engine.

### 273. What should be the main contribution?
1. First signer-independent multimodal benchmark for ISL/WBSL with Bengali translation.
2. A lightweight, real-time edge architecture running on consumer CPUs.
3. A publicly releasable benchmark test set of WBSL regional signs.

### 274. What can realistically be completed within the project timeline?
Data collection of 50–100 signs across 8–10 signers, landmark feature pipeline, deep learning sequence model, and a rule-based or small-LM Gloss-to-Bengali translation module.

### 275. What can realistically be trained with available hardware?
Landmark-based LSTMs and small Transformers (can be trained in hours on Google Colab / local GPU).

### 276. What should be removed if the dataset is insufficient?
Continuous video-to-gloss end-to-end translation (scale back to continuous keyword spotting or isolated sign sequence recognition).

### 277. What should be removed if continuous recognition is too difficult?
Drop unconstrained continuous signing; implement an isolated sign sequence pipeline with explicit pauses.

### 278. What should be removed if Bengali translation is too difficult?
Drop complex generative LLM pipelines in favor of a rule-based/dictionary-based morphological transfer grammar.

### 279. Should isolated-sign recognition be the baseline?
Yes.

### 280. Should continuous sign recognition be the main target?
It should be the primary stretch goal; if time permits, implement continuous sign sequence classification.

### 281. Should ISL-to-Bengali translation be the main target?
Yes, this represents the central research novelty.

### 282. Should fingerspelling recognition be included?
Keep it as an optional modular subsystem for out-of-vocabulary words.

### 283. Should alphabet recognition be included only as a baseline?
Yes. Alphabet recognition should never be the primary deliverable of an advanced thesis.

### 284. Should Bengali text generation be included?
Yes. Translating raw sign glosses into readable Bengali is essential.

### 285. Should Bengali TTS (Text-to-Speech) be included?
Yes. Adding an open-source Bengali TTS engine (e.g., Google TTS or `gTTS`/`espeak-ng`) adds high engineering demonstration value with minimal effort.

### 286. Should Bengali text-to-sign generation be included?
Only as an exploratory future scope; building 3D signing avatars is a distinct subfield.

### 287. Is bidirectional Bengali-to-ISL communication realistic for the project?
No. Bidirectional systems require two complete research architectures; focus exclusively on **ISL-to-Bengali**.

### 288. Should reverse communication be only an exploratory future direction?
Yes. List it in the "Future Work" section.

### 289. Can the project have a useful contribution without solving continuous translation?
Yes. A rigorous, real-time, signer-independent isolated sign translation system with multimodal facial analysis and Bengali generation is a strong academic contribution.

### 290. What hardware is required for the proposed system?
A standard laptop with an Intel i5/AMD Ryzen CPU, 8GB RAM, and a 720p webcam. A dedicated GPU (RTX 3060 or Google Colab T4) is only needed for model training.

### 291. Can the complete system run on CPU?
Yes. MediaPipe Holistic feature extraction combined with an ONNX-quantized LSTM/Transformer runs at 30+ FPS on modern CPUs.

### 292. Can the system run on a low-cost computer?
Yes, when landmark-based models are used instead of raw 3D CNNs.

### 293. Can pose landmarks reduce computational cost?
Yes, by over 95% compared to processing raw 3D video tensors ($543 \text{ coordinates}$ vs. $224 \times 224 \times 3 \text{ pixels}$).

### 294. Can quantisation reduce model size?
Yes. Converting PyTorch models to ONNX / INT8 quantization reduces model file sizes to under 10–20 MB with minimal accuracy loss.

### 295. Can a lightweight model achieve real-time performance?
Yes. Landmark LSTMs have inference latencies under 10–20 milliseconds.

### 296. What is the expected inference latency?
*   Landmark extraction: ~25 ms.
*   LSTM inference: ~5 ms.
*   Total Pipeline Latency: **~30–40 ms per frame**.

### 297. What is the expected memory requirement?
Less than 500 MB RAM during active real-time webcam inference.

### 298. Can the model run on a normal laptop?
Yes.

### 299. What ethical issues exist in collecting Deaf people's videos?
Exploitation, lack of informed consent, cultural misrepresentation, and privacy vulnerabilities of identifiable faces.

### 300. How should consent be obtained from signers?
Through accessible **video-based consent forms presented in ISL** along with written consent, explicitly explaining data usage, licensing, and distribution.

### 301. Can the dataset be publicly released?
Only if signers have given explicit legal consent for public academic distribution.

### 302. How should signer identity and privacy be protected?
By extracting and storing **only the numerical landmark coordinate files (.npy/.csv)** and discarding or blurring the raw RGB video recordings.

### 303. Should raw videos be stored?
Only on secure, encrypted storage accessible strictly to primary researchers; do not upload raw identifiable videos publicly without permission.

### 304. Can landmarks be stored instead of raw video?
Yes. Landmark datasets preserve full kinematics while fully protecting user privacy.

### 305. How can Deaf community members participate in dataset creation?
As paid native consultants, model signers, dataset validators, and co-designers of the user interface.

### 306. How can Deaf signers validate the correctness of the dataset?
By reviewing recorded samples to verify that handshapes, movement trajectories, and non-manual markers meet native linguistic standards.

### 307. How can linguistic mistakes in the dataset be identified?
By running inter-annotator agreement checks (e.g., Cohen’s Kappa) among two or more native Deaf validators.

